{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y66TgG6cXxuZR8DTdLfbVB","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on the context filesystem, I found the answer in the discussion thread for KIP-1163: Diskless Core.\\\\n\\\\n## Answer\\\\n\\\\nThe **list** operation was agreed to be allowed to remain eventually consistent.\\\\n\\\\n## Final Consensus\\\\n\\\\nIn an email from Viktor Somogyi-Vass (dated 2026-04-24), the consensus decision was:\\\\n\\\\n- **put** and **get** operations must be **strongly consistent** (for read-after-write scenarios)\\\\n- **list** operation can remain **eventually consistent**\\\\n\\\\nThe reasoning provided was that the \\\\\\\"list\\\\\\\" operation is only used for garbage collection purposes, where it is acceptable if a few segments are only collected in the next iteration. Since major cloud providers offer strongly consistent object storage, restricting only the \\\\\\\"list\\\\\\\" operation to eventual consistency allows the design to work with a broader range of object storage implementations while keeping complexity minimal.\\\\n\\\\n## Source Files\\\\n\\\\n- `/store/events/2026/04/24/mail-f7476bc5df952b32c1079dd3-158293ff.md` (lines 68-77) - Viktor Somogyi-Vass's response clarifying the consensus on JR7 (consistency requirements)\\\\n- `/store/events/2026/04/21/mail-88e93198e095dbab41373878-b88d024a.md` (lines 159-162) - Jun Rao's original question about which operations can be eventually consistent\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"events/2026/04/20/mail-00dfdc315465260213bf900e-b627a53c.md\\\",\\\"events/2026/04/21/mail-88e93198e095dbab41373878-b88d024a.md\\\",\\\"events/2026/04/24/mail-9fd9164f86d9ca01e49ed6e7-cab13524.md\\\",\\\"events/2026/04/24/mail-f7476bc5df952b32c1079dd3-158293ff.md\\\",\\\"events/2026/04/27/mail-3b03034816281c500f3c3b46-f0d6e998.md\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":23.57,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":1,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\